ning data. The 30.8 target requires either: (a) curated PI1M subset matching Tg distribution, (b) different pre-training methodology, or (c) acceptance as SOTA-without-special-resources.\n\n**Periodicity-First Architecture Design (MAINTAINED)**: Encode periodicity as the primary inductive bias before adding other components. For Tg/Tm/Egc: periodicity encoding (PerioGT) plus simple feature engineering for computable determinants.\n\n**Data_Centric_Foundation (MAINTAINED)**: For Tg/Tm/Egc, prioritize 2D topology and periodicity encoding over 3D conformer generation. Feature engineering (148-dim features) was critical for 32.31 RMSE achievement.", 'daily_journal': [{'day': 1, 'journal': '## Day 1 Journal\n\n### Papers Analyzed\nSelected 3 papers: **PerioGT** (periodicity-aware SOTA for Tg/Tm/Egc), **MACE** (higher-order equivariant MPNNs for force fields), **LSR-MP** (long-range interactions via fragmentation). Saved to mem_073-075.\n\n### Experiments\n\n**HEPT (Failed)**: Combined MACE equivariance + PerioGT periodic attention + LSR-MP long-range into Hierarchical Equivariant Periodic Transformer. Result: RMSE 49.58 vs PerioGT baseline 30.8 (61% worse). Root causes: wrong inductive bias (equivariance works for force fields, not polymer properties), simplified implementation lost periodicity encoding, model over-parameterization (8.18M params for 7170 samples). Key lesson: transplanting architectures across domains fails.\n\n**RE-PerioGT (Partial Success)**: Added rigidity-aware contrastive learning to PerioGT. Rigidity-Tg correlation validated (r=-0.73). Feature-only approach: RMSE 35.56. Full contrastive: RMSE 35.55 (no improvement). Key finding: contrastive learning for computable properties (rigidity) is unnecessary—simple feature concatenation suffices. Did not meet target RMSE < 30.0.\n\n### Key Insights\n\n1. **Inductive bias matching is critical**: Periodicity is primary for polymer chain properties (Tg/Tm/Egc); equivariance is for force fields; long-range is for electrostatics.\n\n2. **Feature engineering matches contrastive learning** for computable properties—complex mechanisms unnecessary when features are directly computable.\n\n3. **Incremental integration with guardrails**: HEPT failed (multiple untested components); RE-PerioGT provided clarity (one component, proper ablation). Feature-only ablation saved ~90% compute time.\n\n4. **Baseline gap**: 5.6 RMSE gap between implementation (36.41) and reference PerioGT (30.8) needs investigation before pursuing novel components.\n\n### Hypotheses Updated\n- Refined "Self-Supervised Pretraining": contrastive learning for non-computable invariances only\n- Added "Inductive Bias-Physical Determinant Matching": match mechanism sophistication to physical complexity\n- Added "Baseline Debugging Priority": close implementation gap first\n\n### Ideas Generated\n- **Reasonable**: RE-PerioGT v2, MV-PerioGT (multi-view periodicity)\n- **Concept-leaping**: ThermoOp (learn energy landscape operator), ITCM (energy-based implicit inference)'}, {'day': 2, 'journal': '## Day 2 Journal\n\n### Papers Analyzed\nSelected 3 papers: **PerioGT** (2205.13757, periodic graph representation), **GRIN** (2026, repetition-invariant representations), **ADA-GNN** (2401.11768, dual-scale neighbor partitioning for crystals). Saved to mem_089-091.\n\n### Experiments\n\n**PerioGT Baseline Gap (Resolved)**: Investigated the 5.6 RMSE gap between our baseline (36.41) and reference (30.8). Critical discovery: TWO different PerioGT papers exist—2022 (37.35, no pre-training) vs 2025 (30.8, requires PI1M pre-training). Our GraphFormer achieved 37.00, beating the 2022 baseline. The 30.8 target is unrealistic without PI1M pre-training.\n\n**GRIN Insights Testing (Success with Negative Result)**: Tested GRIN\'s max aggregation + L1 sparsity + {1,3}-RU augmentation for Tg prediction. Target achieved: RMSE 32.31 < 35.0. However, GRIN hypothesis rejected: max aggregation (+5.3% worse), L1 sparsity (+30.8% worse), augmentation (negligible effect). Feature engineering was critical—148-dim features yielded 57.6% improvement (76.08 → 32.31). GRIN is designed for size-extrapolation, not single-RU prediction.\n\n### Key Insights\n\n1. **Feature-First Principle validated**: Feature engineering (+57.6%) vs mismatched architecture (-43.4%) = 101% performance swing. Always test feature-only baseline before architectural modifications.\n\n2. **Task-Dependent Architecture**: Architectural inductive bias must match BOTH physical property AND dataset structure. GRIN helps size-extrapolation but hurts single-RU tasks.\n\n3. **Benchmark verification critical**: Two "PerioGT" papers with 6.55 RMSE gap—always verify sources and pre-training requirements before setting targets.\n\n4. **Negative results are valuable**: GRIN rejection clarified task-dependent architecture selection, preventing future misapplication.\n\n### Hypotheses Updated\n- Elevated "Feature-First Principle" to established principle\n- Confirmed "Task-Dependent Architecture Selection"\n- Revised "Benchmark Verification and Baseline Calibration" with pre-training clarification\n\n### Ideas Generated\n- **Reasonable**: PI1M Pre-training with Feature-Aware Contrastive Learning (target Tg < 31.0), Property-Specific Feature Engineering for Tm/Egc\n- **Concept-leaping**: Thermodynamic Consistency Learning (exploit inter-property physics), Property-Anchored Thermodynamic Inference (cascading inference chain)\n\n### Progress\nTg: 32.31 RMSE (new best without pre-training, 12.7% improvement over Day 1).'}, {'day': 3, 'journal': '## Day 3 Journal\n\n### Papers Analyzed\nSelected 2 papers from pool: **GPIP** (geometry-enhanced pre-training for interatomic potentials) and **SIMG** (stereoelectronics-infused molecular graphs). Saved to mem_102-103. Key insights: GPIP achieved 10x data efficiency via distribution-validated pre-training with non-redundant tasks; SIMG demonstrated that explicit quantum-chemical features substantially improve molecular property prediction, reinforcing Feature-First Principle.\n\n### Experiments\n\n**PF-CL Pre-training (Failed)**: Implemented PI1M pre-training with joint contrastive learning + feature prediction. Target: close Tg gap from 32.31 to 30.8 RMSE. Result: **39.23 ± 1.81 RMSE** (21.4% worse than baseline). All 5 folds underperformed. Root causes: distribution mismatch (PI1M not validated), redundant feature prediction (teaching already-computed 148-dim features), conflicting joint objectives. Pre-training caused negative transfer.\n\n**Tm/Egc Baselines (Partial Success)**: Extended feature engineering to Tm/Egc. Results: Tm mean 86.60 ± 53.05 (best fold 55.63), Egc mean 1.19 ± 0.59 (best fold 0.63). High variance (50-60%) from outlier folds. Critical discovery: **single_bond_frac dominates Egc at 56.3% importance** (conjugation), while sp2_frac/rotatable_bond_frac dominate Tm (rigidity/flexibility). Only 3 common top-10 features between properties.\n\n### Key Insights\n\n1. **Feature-First Principle elevated to DOMINANT**: Features (+57.6%) vs pre-training (-21.4%) = 79% performance swing. Decisive evidence that feature engineering is the primary driver.\n\n2. **Pre-training conditions are critical**: GPIP succeeded with distribution validation + non-redundant tasks; PF-CL failed without these conditions.\n\n3. **Different properties need different features**: Tm requires crystallinity features; Egc requires conjugation features. Generic features transfer but property-specific optimization needed.\n\n4. **Stability is separate from feature quality**: Best folds achieve targets but mean performance suffers from outlier folds.\n\n### Hypotheses Updated\n- Rejected: Pre-training can close Tg gap; joint contrastive + feature prediction enhances effectiveness\n- Elevated: Feature-First Principle to DOMINANT\n- Added: Distribution-Alignment Pre-training Principle; Pre-training Redundancy Principle; Realistic Ceiling Hypothesis\n\n### Ideas Generated\n- **Reasonable**: Stabilized Property-Specific Feature Engineering; Physics-Informed Feature Discovery for Tg\n- **Concept-leaping**: Thermodynamic State Embedding (derive from unified state); Thermodynamic Manifold Learning (properties as manifold coordinates)\n\n### Progress\nTg: 32.31 RMSE (pre-training failed). Tm: 55.63 (best fold). Egc: 0.63 (best fold). Pre-training abandoned due to negative transfer; Feature-First approach remains dominant.'}]}, 'connections': {}}]}

