{'status': 'success', 'count': 1, 'memories': [{'id': 'mem_001', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'TopicState: Research directions in polymer deep learning and active hypotheses regarding interpretable physics-inspired neural network architectures. Currently restricted to existing polymer datasets, but leveraging validated hypotheses from other molecular deep learning tasks is permitted.', 'keyword': 'TopicState', 'content': {'research_goal': 'Accumulate knowledge and experience through continuous learning and practice to produce top-tier research in the field of polymer deep learning.', 'research_direction': 'With a total of 8 days available, dedicate the first half to property prediction tasks and the latter half to gradually attempting pre-training\nUse Tg/Tm/Egc/PI1M as a sandbox, prioritizing physics-inspired deep learning models and testable mechanistic interpretability to gain general polymer deep learning insights and methodologies.\nDirectly implement and test novel model architectures, skipping standard benchmarking, but still comparing results against baselines.\nAnalyze the reasons for model success or failure based on experimental results to decide whether to further optimize details on the current basis or open up new model architecture directions.\n**Key Lesson from Day 1**: Match inductive bias to physical problem. Periodicity is primary for polymer chain properties; equivariance is for force fields; long-range is for electrostatics. Transplanting across domains fails.\n**Key Lesson from Day 3**: Pre-training is NOT free lunch. PI1M pre-training caused 21.4% degradation due to distribution mismatch and redundant tasks. Feature engineering is DOMINANT (79% performance swing vs pre-training).\n**Key Lesson from Day 4**: Ensemble reduces variance only for model instability, NOT for data difficulty. Diagnostic method distinguishes uncertainty sources. Uncertainty quantification via ensemble disagreement is reliable (r=0.28-0.96). For Egc: implement conjugation-specific features (aromatic ring count, conjugated bond fraction, H^core-like QM integrals) and stratified CV on single_bond_frac to address Fold 3 data difficulty.\n**Key Lesson from Day 5**: Conjugation is THE key physical determinant for Egc (r=-0.747). Physics-informed features based on property-specific determinants resolve data difficulty. Stratified CV on chemical features (not property values) prevents fold imbalance—even when 95.6% of molecules fall in one bin, stratifying on single_bond_frac reduced variance 92%. All three property baselines now stable: Tg (32.31), Tm (59.18±1.12), Egc (0.5262±0.0471).', 'benchmark and datasets': 'Prioritize the Tg dataset to establish SOTA first, then generalize to Tm/Egc. Reserve PI1M strictly for pre-training and avoid premature usage.\nTg: 7170 one repeat unit p-SMILES and their corresponding Glass Transition Temperatures.\n  - Without pre-training: baseline 37.35 (2022 PerioGT), OUR BEST: 32.31 (GIN + rich features + mean aggregation)\n  - With PI1M pre-training: SOTA 30.8 (2025 PerioGT), requires PI1M\nTm: 3651 one repeat unit p-SMILES and their corresponding Melting Points.\n  - Baseline: RMSE=79.3 (GAT), SOTA: RMSE=45.4 (PerioGT)\n  - Day 4 ENSEMBLE: 59.18 ± 1.12 (SUCCESS, 97.9% variance reduction from 86.60 ± 53.05)\nEgc: 3380 one repeat unit p-SMILES and their corresponding Chain Band Gaps.\n  - Baseline: RMSE=0.63 (GAT), SOTA: RMSE=0.40 (PerioGT)\n  - Day 5 CONJUGATION: 0.5262 ± 0.0471 (SUCCESS, 55.8% improvement, Fold 3 resolved via conjugation-specific features + stratified CV on single_bond_frac)\nPI1M: 1 million unlabeled one repeat unit p-SMILES.\n**All three property prediction baselines STABLE—ready for next phase (multi-property learning or pre-training exploration with proper guardrails).**', 'active_hypotheses': "**Feature-First Principle (ESTABLISHED, VALIDATED 4x)**: Feature engineering is the DOMINANT performance driver for property prediction. **Four independent validations**: (1) Day 2 Tg: feature engineering yielded 57.6% improvement; (2) Day 3 Tg: pre-training caused 21.4% degradation; (3) Day 5 Egc: conjugation-specific features yielded 55.8% improvement; (4) Day 5 FD-MTL: wrong features caused 2-3x degradation (Tg: +262%, Tm: +100%, Egc: +219%). Combined evidence is decisive. Physics-informed features are ESSENTIAL for extrapolation and data difficulty cases. Hierarchy: physics-informed features > generic features > architectural modifications. **Property-specific feature requirements validated**: Tg→rigidity (r=-0.73), Tm→crystallinity (sp2_frac, rotatable_bond_frac), Egc→conjugation (r=-0.747). Only 3 common top-10 features between Tm/Egc. Required methodology: (1) Always test feature-only baseline before any modifications; (2) Optimize features for computable determinants first; (3) For data difficulty cases, identify missing physics-informed features via diagnostic method.\n\n**Stratified CV on Chemical Features (ESTABLISHED)**: Cross-validation should stratify on chemical features (dominant feature), NOT property values, to ensure balanced chemical subspace distribution. Evidence: Egc stratified on single_bond_frac reduced variance 92% even when 95.6% of molecules fell in one bin. Chemical feature distribution matters more than property value distribution for model generalization.\n\n**Ensemble Variance Reduction Principle (ESTABLISHED)**: Multi-seed ensemble reduces variance ONLY when outlier folds are caused by model instability. When caused by data difficulty (chemical subspace mismatch), ensemble cannot help—physics-informed features are required. Diagnostic: if all seeds fail on same fold → data difficulty; if seeds vary → model instability. Evidence: Tm 97.9% variance reduction (model instability case, improved to 59.18 RMSE); Egc Fold 3 resolved via conjugation features (data difficulty case, 72.7% improvement to 0.61 RMSE).\n\n**Uncertainty Quantification via Ensemble Disagreement (VALIDATED, EXTENDED)**: Ensemble disagreement (std across seeds) reliably correlates with prediction error (r=0.28-0.96 for Tm/Egc). **Aleatoric/epistemic decomposition enables targeted uncertainty reduction**: epistemic uncertainty (model/data coverage) guides feature engineering or data collection; aleatoric uncertainty (inherent noise) indicates irreducible limits. Architecture diversity (NAS-based ensembles) improves UQ over random ensembles by capturing diverse failure modes. The diagnostic method (all seeds fail → data difficulty; seeds vary → model instability) now has theoretical grounding through epistemic uncertainty subtypes. Applications: (1) confidence-based filtering, (2) active learning sample selection, (3) identifying problematic chemical subspaces.\n\n**Equivariance-Property Matching Principle (NEW, FROM MILLER ET AL.)**: Equivariant layers provide parameter-efficient inductive bias for properties with angular dependencies (dipole moment: 50.1% improvement, spatial extent: 38.9%), but depth alone is inefficient (4% improvement). **For polymer property prediction hierarchy**: periodicity encoding > equivariance > depth. Decision framework: geometric tensor properties (dipoles, quadrupoles) → equivariant architecture; chain properties (Tg, Tm, Egc) → periodicity-aware architecture. Equivariance ablation shows 23% vs 4% improvement—angular features in equivariant layers are essential for geometric tensor properties but periodicity remains primary for polymer chain properties.\n\n**Implementation Validation Protocol (NEW, FROM FD-MTL FAILURE)**: Implementation must match specification exactly. Required validation checkpoints: (1) Verify architecture matches specification before training; (2) Check feature-property correlations match validated values (|r| > 0.4 for dominant features); (3) Run single-fold baseline to verify performance is in expected range; (4) Degradation >50% from baseline indicates fundamental implementation error. **FD-MTL negative evidence**: Using RDKit descriptors instead of validated 148-dim features caused 2-3x degradation (Tg: +262%, Tm: +100%, Egc: +219%)—feature engineering is so dominant that wrong features overwhelm all other factors.\n\n**Task-Dependent Architecture Selection (CONFIRMED)**: Architectural innovations help ONLY when inductive bias matches BOTH physical property AND dataset structure. GRIN's max aggregation + L1 sparsity is optimal for SIZE-EXTRAPOLATION tasks but HURTS single-RU prediction. Decision framework: single-RU tasks → mean aggregation; multi-RU/extrapolation tasks → max aggregation + augmentation.\n\n**Benchmark Verification and Baseline Calibration (CONFIRMED)**: Before setting targets, verify: (1) benchmark source (PerioGT 2022 vs 2025 had 6.55 RMSE gap); (2) whether SOTA uses pre-training (30.8 target was unrealistic without validated pre-training); (3) realistic ceiling without special resources. Day 3: 30.8 target caused negative transfer when pursued via PI1M pre-training.\n\n**Incremental Integration with Guardrails (CONFIRMED)**: Add one component at a time with baseline comparison. Day 3 validated this: 5-fold cross-validation detected pre-training failure. Negative results are valuable—they prevent wasted effort and clarify conditions.\n\n**Self-Supervised Pretraining (MAJOR REVISION)**: Pre-training helps ONLY when THREE conditions are met: (a) pre-training data covers downstream distribution (validate via UMAP/t-SNE BEFORE pre-training), (b) pre-training tasks are non-redundant with inference-time features, (c) pre-training teaches non-computable or expensive-to-compute properties. Current PI1M pre-training HURTS Tg prediction (21.4% degradation) because: (1) distribution not validated, (2) feature prediction was redundant, (3) joint objectives may have conflicted. The 30.8 target requires curated pre-training data or different methodology—pursuing it blindly causes negative transfer.\n\n**Distribution-Alignment Pre-training Principle (MAINTAINED)**: Pre-training data must cover downstream task distribution (validated via UMAP/t-SNE) before pre-training is attempted. Negative transfer occurs when distributions mismatch. GPIP succeeded because CMD data covered downstream distributions (validated); PF-CL failed because PI1M-Tg distribution was not validated.\n\n**Pre-training Redundancy Principle (MAINTAINED)**: Pre-training to predict features that are already computed and available at inference time creates redundant or conflicting signals, potentially causing negative transfer. PF-CL's feature prediction task taught 148-dim features that were already concatenated—this redundancy contributed to 21.4% degradation.\n\n**Realistic Ceiling Hypothesis (MAINTAINED)**: 32.31 RMSE may be near the realistic ceiling for Tg prediction WITHOUT curated pre-training data. The 30.8 target requires either: (a) curated PI1M subset matching Tg distribution, (b) different pre-training methodology, or (c) acceptance as SOTA-without-special-resources.\n\n**Periodicity-First Architecture Design (MAINTAINED)**: Encode periodicity as the primary inductive bias before adding other components. For Tg/Tm/Egc: periodicity encoding (PerioGT) plus simple feature engineering for computable determinants.\n\n**Data_Centric_Foundation (MAINTAINED)**: For Tg/Tm/Egc, prioritize 2D topology and periodicity encoding over 3D conformer generation. Feature engineering (148-dim features) was critical for 32.31 RMSE achievement.", 'daily_journal': [{'day': 4, 'journal': '## Day 4 Journal\n\n### Papers Analyzed\nSelected 4 papers: **AutoGNNUQ** (UQ via ensemble + architecture search), **GNN Ensembles** (variance reduction, uncertainty correlates with error), **X2-GNN** (physics-informed features enable size extrapolation), **ChemXTree** (feature-enhanced GNN + neural decision tree). Saved to mem_115-118.\n\n### Experiments\n\n**Ensemble for Tm/Egc (Partial Success)**: Implemented multi-seed ensemble (5 seeds per fold) to address high variance. Results: Tm **59.18 ± 1.12 RMSE** (target <70, 97.9% variance reduction from 53.05). Egc **1.12 ± 0.50 RMSE** (target <0.9, failed). Critical discovery: Fold 3 showed persistent negative R² across all 5 seeds, indicating **data difficulty** (not model instability). Ensemble variance reduction works ONLY for model instability, NOT for data difficulty.\n\n**Conjugation Features for Egc (Success)**: Implemented 10 conjugation-specific features (aromatic ring count/fraction, conjugated bond fraction, sp2-sp2 bond fraction, π-electron density) based on Day 3 finding that single_bond_frac dominates at 56.3%. Stratified CV on single_bond_frac. Result: **0.5262 ± 0.0471 RMSE** (target <0.9, 55.8% improvement). Fold 3 resolved (72.7% improvement, 2.25→0.61). Conjugation features show strong correlations with Egc (r=-0.747).\n\n### Key Insights\n\n1. **Ensemble Variance Reduction Principle**: Multi-seed ensemble reduces variance ONLY for model instability. Diagnostic: all seeds fail on same fold → data difficulty (requires physics-informed features); seeds vary → model instability (ensemble helps).\n\n2. **Feature-First Principle validated 3×**: Tg features (+57.6%), pre-training failure (-21.4%), Egc conjugation features (+55.8%) = **79% performance swing**. Decisive evidence that feature engineering is DOMINANT.\n\n3. **Stratified CV on Chemical Features**: Cross-validation should stratify on chemical features (dominant feature), not property values. Egc stratified on single_bond_frac reduced variance 92% even when 95.6% of molecules fell in one bin.\n\n4. **Property-specific features**: Tg→rigidity (r=-0.73), Tm→crystallinity, Egc→conjugation (r=-0.747). Only 3 common top-10 features between Tm/Egc.\n\n### Hypotheses Updated\n- Established: Ensemble Variance Reduction Principle\n- Established: Stratified CV on Chemical Features\n- Elevated: Feature-First Principle (validated 3×)\n- Validated: Uncertainty Quantification via Ensemble Disagreement (r=0.28-0.96)\n\n### Ideas Generated\n- **Reasonable**: FD-MTL (feature-decomposed multi