tation must match specification exactly. Required validation checkpoints: (1) Verify architecture matches specification before training; (2) Check feature-property correlations match validated values (|r| > 0.4 for dominant features); (3) Run single-fold baseline to verify performance is in expected range; (4) Degradation >50% from baseline indicates fundamental implementation error. **Day 5-6 evidence is decisive**: wrong features (r<0.05) caused 2-3x degradation, corrected features (r>0.4) enabled positive MTL transfer. Feature engineering is so dominant that wrong features overwhelm all other factors.\n\n**Architecture-Property Matching for Thermal Transitions (NEW, Day 6)**: Tg prediction needs deeper architecture or complete features. Evidence: CFD-MTL achieved baseline-matching for Tm and Egc, but Tg underperformed by 36% (44.12 vs 32.31). Possible explanations: (1) Simplified 3-layer GIN vs 5-layer baseline insufficient for thermal transition physics; (2) Reduced 80 features vs 148 missing critical rigidity determinants; (3) Thermal transition involves more complex multi-scale physics than melting or electronic transition. **Property-type differences**: intensive properties (Egc) benefit more from shared learning (17% improvement) than extensive properties (Tg: 3%, Tm: 9%). MBNN-att suggests property-type-aware attention: softmax for intensive, sigmoid additive for extensive.\n\n**Stratified CV on Chemical Features (ESTABLISHED)**: Cross-validation should stratify on chemical features (dominant feature), NOT property values, to ensure balanced chemical subspace distribution. Evidence: Egc stratified on single_bond_frac reduced variance 92% even when 95.6% of molecules fell in one bin. Chemical feature distribution matters more than property value distribution for model generalization.\n\n**Ensemble Variance Reduction Principle (ESTABLISHED)**: Multi-seed ensemble reduces variance ONLY when outlier folds are caused by model instability. When caused by data dif