 Selection (CONFIRMED)**: Architectural innovations help ONLY when inductive bias matches BOTH physical property AND dataset structure. GRIN's max aggregation + L1 sparsity is optimal for SIZE-EXTRAPOLATION tasks but HURTS single-RU prediction. Decision framework: single-RU tasks → mean aggregation; multi-RU/extrapolation tasks → max aggregation + augmentation.\n\n**Benchmark Verification and Baseline Calibration (CONFIRMED)**: Before setting targets, verify: (1) benchmark source (PerioGT 2022 vs 2025 had 6.55 RMSE gap); (2) whether SOTA uses pre-training (30.8 target was unrealistic without validated pre-training); (3) realistic ceiling without special resources. Day 3: 30.8 target caused negative transfer when pursued via PI1M pre-training.\n\n**Incremental Integration with Guardrails (CONFIRMED)**: Add one component at a time with baseline comparison. Day 3 validated this: 5-fold cross-validation detected pre-training failure. Negative results are valuable—they prevent wasted effort and clarify conditions.\n\n**Self-Supervised Pretraining (MAJOR REVISION)**: Pre-training helps ONLY when THREE conditions are met: (a) pre-training data covers downstream distribution (validate via UMAP/t-SNE BEFORE pre-training), (b) pre-training tasks are non-redundant with inference-time features, (c) pre-training teaches non-computable or expensive-to-compute properties. Current PI1M pre-training HURTS Tg prediction (21.4% degradation) because: (1) distribution not validated, (2) feature prediction was redundant, (3) joint objectives may have conflicted. The 30.8 target requires curated pre-training data or different methodology—pursuing it blindly causes negative transfer.\n\n**Distribution-Alignment Pre-training Principle (MAINTAINED)**: Pre-training data must cover downstream task distribution (validated via UMAP/t-SNE) before pre-training is attempted. Negative transfer occurs when distributions mismatch. GPIP succeeded because CMD data covered downstream distributions (validated); PF-CL failed because PI1M-Tg distribution was not validated.\n\n**Pre-training Redundancy Principle (MAINTAINED)**: Pre-training to predict features that are already computed and available at inference time creates redundant or conflicting signals, potentially causing negative transfer. PF-CL's feature prediction task taught 148-dim features that were already concatenated—this redundancy contributed to 21.4% degradation.\n\n**Realistic Ceiling Hypothesis (MAINTAINED)**: 32.31 RMSE may be near the realistic ceiling for Tg prediction WITHOUT curated pre-training data. The 30.8 target requires either: (a) curated PI1M subset matching Tg distribution, (b) different pre-training methodology, or (c) acceptance as SOTA-without-special-resources.\n\n**Periodicity-First Architecture Design (MAINTAINED)**: Encode periodicity as the primary inductive bias before adding other components. For Tg/Tm/Egc: periodicity encoding (PerioGT) plus simple feature engineering for computable determinants.\n\n**Data_Centric_Foundation (MAINTAINED)**: For Tg/Tm/Egc, prioritize 2D topology and periodicity encoding over 3D conformer generation. Feature engineering (148-dim features) was critical for 32.31 RMSE achievement.", 'daily_journal': [{'day': 6, 'journal': "## Day 6 Journal\n\n### Papers Analyzed\nSelected 2 papers: **PointGAT** (3D geometry + graph attention for QM properties, 10.7% MAE reduction from 3D features), **MBNN-att** (property-type-aware attention: softmax for intensive properties, sigmoid for extensive). Saved to mem_145-146.\n\n### Experiments\n\n**CFD-MTL (PARTIAL SUCCESS)**: Corrected implementation of feature-decomposed multi-task learning with validated 80-dim features. Results: **Tm 59.93±2.71** (matches baseline 59.18±1.12), **Egc 0.552±0.0406** (matches baseline 0.5262±0.0471), **Tg 44.12±1.07** (36% worse than baseline 32.31). Feature decomposition enables positive transfer for ALL properties (Tg: 3%, Tm: 9%, Egc: 17% improvement from MTL). **Day 5 catastrop